WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:01.453
- [Instructor] In other videos,

00:00:01.453 --> 00:00:06.453
we've already introduced the
idea of an extraneous solution

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.060
where you go about solving an equation.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:11.600
So you're given an original equation,

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:13.830
and you do a bunch of algebraic steps,

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:18.240
and then you solve it, and
you get some solutions.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:19.750
And what we've seen, especially when

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.480
we're dealing with radical equations

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.510
and especially some rational equations,

00:00:23.510 --> 00:00:27.160
sometimes all of the
solutions don't work for

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:28.450
our original equation.

00:00:28.450 --> 00:00:31.160
And so the solutions that don't work,

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.950
that don't work, these are the ones

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:38.190
that we call extraneous.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.200
What we're going to do in this video is

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.410
get a little bit deeper.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.040
We're gonna look at some
extraneous solutions,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.150
but really think deeply
about why they show up.

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:48.640
So let's just give an example,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.020
and this is one that hopefully
you're familiar with,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:53.260
or at least the idea of
it you're familiar with,

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:54.810
when you're dealing
with radical equations.

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.150
Let's say you're trying to
solve two x minus one is equal

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:00.750
to the square root of eight minus x.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:01.920
And I'm gonna go quickly through it.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.690
If this is unfamiliar to you, I encourage

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.340
you to review solving radical equations.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:08.880
But we're gonna really study why

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.470
we get an extraneous solution here.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.680
So, actually, why don't
you pause this video.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:13.760
Figure out what the solutions are.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:14.593
I'll give you a hint.

00:01:14.593 --> 00:01:16.030
One of them's going to be extraneous.

00:01:16.030 --> 00:01:17.520
And really think about why

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.323
that extraneous solution shows up.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.630
All right, now let's do it together

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.860
and just to go through the steps that

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.270
we've seen before for radical equations.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.320
You wanna square both sides of this.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.860
So if you square the left-hand side,

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.882
you'll get four x squared, I should say,

00:01:33.882 --> 00:01:37.500
minus four x plus one is equal to,

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.860
you square the square
root of eight minus x.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.800
You're going to get just eight minus x.

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:42.860
And now let's see.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:46.000
We can subtract eight from both sides,

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.900
and we can add x to both sides,

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.320
so we just have zero
on the right-hand side.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:53.190
And on the left-hand side we're going

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:58.190
to get four x squared minus
three x minus seven is equal

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.530
to zero, so we have an
interesting quadratic here.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:03.880
You could use the quadratic formula,

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.870
but it looks like this one
might be factorable by grouping.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.292
We just have to think of two numbers

00:02:08.292 --> 00:02:12.470
that, when we add them, so a plus b needs

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:14.890
to be equal to negative three,

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.720
and a times b needs to be equal

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:18.110
to four times negative seven,

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.970
needs to be equal to negative 28.

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:24.500
And the obvious ones seem
to be right in front of us,

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.270
four and negative seven.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:29.050
Four plus negative seven
is equal to negative three.

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.840
Four times negative seven
is equal to negative 28.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.520
So we can just break
this negative three up

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.370
into, or negative three x up into a four x

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:39.670
and a negative seven x.

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:40.540
So let me do that.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.690
So you have four x squared,
and then I'm just going to,

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:45.190
and this is just factoring by grouping.

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:46.870
Once again, if this is
unfamiliar, I encourage

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.780
you to review that on Khan Academy.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:51.880
So we're going to get
plus four x minus seven x,

00:02:51.880 --> 00:02:54.240
and notice these two add
up to negative three x.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.750
So I'm not changing the equation, really,

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.451
or the value, or what
it's trying to express,

00:02:58.451 --> 00:03:00.580
is equal to zero.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.560
And in factoring by grouping we can group

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.460
these two together, and then we can group

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.080
these two together.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.650
And for these first two, we can factor

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.410
out a four x, and so you're going

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:17.490
to get a four x times x plus one.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.420
And in these second two we can factor

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:24.050
out a negative seven, minus
seven times x plus one,

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.860
and all of that is going
to be equal to zero.

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:30.210
And then we can factor out an x plus one.

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:33.420
And so we're going to get x plus one

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:36.480
times four x minus seven.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.480
So times four x minus
seven is equal to zero.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:45.070
And once again, this is just to get us

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:47.340
to a place where we can look
at what the solutions are.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.520
So we can see that either
x plus one is going

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.210
to be equal to zero, which happens

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.190
when x is equal to negative one,

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:58.320
or four x minus seven is equal to zero,

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.550
which happens when four
x is equal to seven,

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.610
which happens when x is equal to 7/4.

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.080
So we've got these two
solutions and I promised you

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:09.160
that one of them would be extraneous.

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:12.450
So let's try this first
one, x equals negative one.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.220
And remember, when you solve
a radical equation like this

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:17.190
you wanna substitute back
into the original equation

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.739
to see whether they're extraneous or not.

00:04:18.739 --> 00:04:21.710
So when you substitute x
equals negative one back here,

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:25.380
you're going to get two times negative one

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.180
minus one is going to be
equal to the square root

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.800
of eight minus negative one,

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.420
and so on the left-hand side

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.840
you have negative two minus one.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.850
That is negative three.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:42.620
And you get that equaling
the square root of nine,

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:46.070
the principal root of nine, and
so you get this weird result

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:48.290
that negative three is equal to three,

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:50.320
which we know is not
the case, and that's why

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.370
we know this is extraneous.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:55.350
But as I promised, why did this happen?

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.420
And the key is to realize that

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.550
when you're doing algebraic manipulation,

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:01.322
some operations are reversible,

00:05:01.322 --> 00:05:03.220
and some are not.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:07.570
For example, if you say
that a plus b is equal

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.980
to c, and you wanna solve for a,

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:13.430
and you subtract b from both sides,

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:17.132
you are going to get a
is equal to c minus b.

00:05:17.132 --> 00:05:19.760
And if you were to start
off with a is equal

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:21.900
to c minus b, you could add b

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.020
to both sides.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:25.500
You could add b to both sides to get back

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:26.730
to your original equation,

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:29.680
to get a plus b is equal to c.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.130
So this adding and subtracting values

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:34.640
from both sides, that is
a reversible operation.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.140
You can do it in either direction.

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:39.710
But when you're squaring things,

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:43.557
that is not true, and we
did square right over here.

00:05:43.557 --> 00:05:48.170
To go from that first step
to this step, we squared.

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:50.470
And what we'll see, or what
you might already recognize is

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:53.060
that squaring is not reversible.

00:05:53.060 --> 00:05:55.597
If I know that a is equal to to b,

00:05:55.597 --> 00:05:58.110
and then I were to square it, we do know

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.690
that a squared is equal to to b squared.

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:01.910
But if we go the other way around,

00:06:01.910 --> 00:06:04.110
if we know that a squared
is equal to b squared,

00:06:04.110 --> 00:06:06.720
so if we know that a squared
is equal to b squared

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:08.470
do we know that it's always the case

00:06:08.470 --> 00:06:11.130
that a is equal to b?

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.113
Well I'll give you an example that shows

00:06:12.113 --> 00:06:14.430
that that is not always true.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:16.560
We know that negative two squared is equal

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.560
to two squared, but negative
two is not equal to two.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.160
So that's what we did here.

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:27.290
We squared at this step,
and it's not reversible,

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:29.030
and we were able to do a bunch of stuff,

00:06:29.030 --> 00:06:30.390
and we got all the way to the point

00:06:30.390 --> 00:06:32.360
that saying, okay, x is
equal to negative one,

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:36.620
or x is equal to 7/4, but
it's not always the case

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:38.060
that when x equals negative one

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:39.470
that we can reverse all the way back

00:06:39.470 --> 00:06:42.370
to this original equation and
that two x minus one is equal

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:45.620
to the square root of eight minus x.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:48.130
And the two places where this is important

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:49.370
when you're dealing with algebra,

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:51.480
the two obvious places
are when you're dealing

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:53.125
with radical equations,
'cause you're doing

00:06:53.125 --> 00:06:56.580
that squaring step, and that squaring step

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:57.910
is not reversible.

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:00.159
The other case is when you are dealing

00:07:00.159 --> 00:07:04.100
with multiplying both sides by a variable.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:06.650
And to understand that, if we know

00:07:06.650 --> 00:07:09.110
that a is equal to to b, but then

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:13.030
we were to multiply both sides
of this equation times x.

00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:15.403
And so we'll get xa is equal to xb.

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.071
That could be true, but is the other way

00:07:20.071 --> 00:07:21.970
around necessarily true?

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:24.879
If we know that xa is equal to xb,

00:07:24.879 --> 00:07:29.158
is it always the case
that a is equal to b?

00:07:29.158 --> 00:07:30.890
Well, I'll give you an example where

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:31.723
that isn't the case.

00:07:31.723 --> 00:07:34.340
What happens when x is equal to zero?

00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:39.340
We know that zero times three
is equal to zero times four,

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.300
but it is not the case that
three is equal to four.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:45.861
And so this is another situation

00:07:45.861 --> 00:07:48.749
where multiplying both
sides of an equation

00:07:48.749 --> 00:07:52.085
by a variable, you can
go in that direction,

00:07:52.085 --> 00:07:54.760
but it's not necessarily reversible.

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.930
You can't necessarily go
back in that direction.

00:07:57.930 --> 00:08:00.710
And that's why when you solve an equation,

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:03.950
oftentimes a rational equation,
by multiplying both sides

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:06.730
of that equation by a
variable and you get a result,

00:08:06.730 --> 00:08:08.370
it's not necessarily
true that you could take

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:10.010
that result and reverse everything

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:11.860
and get back to your original equation.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:14.840
And that's why you have to
check your extraneous solutions,

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:16.490
especially for radical equations,

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:19.180
and especially when you
do an algebraic operation

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:21.497
like multiplying both sides of an equation

00:08:21.497 --> 00:08:23.740
by a variable or by an expression

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:25.233
that involves a variable.

